data: {"invocationId":"e-a5d218f5-fb1f-4770-9b60-0e156ab54f34","author":"crucible","actions":{"stateDelta":{"intake":{"question":"Should we replace our front-desk phone line with an AI receptionist that answers, books appointments, and records calls?","leaning":"Yes — sign up for an AI receptionist this month","profile":{"name":"Bright Smile Family Dental","industry":"dental practice","location":"Silver Spring, Maryland","size":"2 dentists, 6 staff","current_tools":["Dentrix","Google Workspace","RingCentral"],"budget":"$400/month","goals":["stop missing new-patient calls","free up front-desk time"],"constraints":"Front desk is one person"}},"evidence_mode":"web"},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":"Assemble the shared evidence for this decision: Should we replace our front-desk phone line with an AI receptionist that answers, books appointments, and records calls? (dental practice, Silver Spring, Maryland)","nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/intake@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/intake@1"]},"id":"2ce56bbc-62c7-40a2-949e-47b23cfc4cc0","timestamp":1794029298.9340727}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-sonnet-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"I'm checking the output format: it has to be a single line of JSON, with the claim as plain text and no link or citation markup in it. The URLs go only in the source field, which means the system's request for markdown citations gives way to the schema here. Next I'm working on the Maryland legal point, that it's an all-party consent state, and I'm drawing that from general knowledge.\n\nNext I'm running through the Maryland and federal rules that could apply to a Silver Spring dental office, like the all-party recording consent law and the HIPAA business associate requirement. Pricing and the names of the AI receptionist vendors are still unverified, so I'm marking those as approximate.\n\nI'm working out the remaining points: the options to lay out, from doing nothing up to a pilot, a full replacement, or a part-time live answering service, along with a list of open questions. For Dentrix, I'm noting that integration goes through the partner program and needs checking against the version the office runs.\n\n","thought":true},{"text":"{\"decision\":\"Whether Bright Smile Family Dental should route its main phone line to an AI receptionist that answers, books into the schedule and records calls, instead of the current front-desk phone line.\",\"options\":[\"Do nothing: keep the current front-desk line and RingCentral setup as is.\",\"Run a limited two-week AI pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with staff still answering the main line, then decide.\",\"Replace the front-desk line with an AI receptionist for all calls, with a human handoff for urgent or clinical calls.\",\"Use a live or hybrid answering service (agents, optionally with AI first) for overflow and after-hours instead of a full AI receptionist.\"],\"facts\":[{\"claim\":\"Maryland is generally treated as an all-party-consent state for recording calls (Maryland Wiretap Act, Courts and Judicial Proceedings 10-402), so a recorded call usually needs every party's consent. This is why a greeting that discloses recording is needed on every route (daytime, overflow, after-hours, transfers). Counsel should confirm.\",\"source\":\"\",\"verified\":false},{\"claim\":\"Legend NT advises having counsel approve the greeting, any 'automated assistant' notice and the recording process before go-live, because recording rules differ by state and some states require all parties to consent.\",\"source\":\"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"If a vendor hears, records, transcribes or stores patient information for the practice, HIPAA generally requires a signed business associate agreement (BAA). Legend NT advises confirming the BAA covers the exact plan being purchased, not only a higher tier, and getting the signed copy before the first live call.\",\"source\":\"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"Legend NT lists data questions to put in writing: who the sub-processors are (speech-to-text, language model, hosting), whether recordings or transcripts train any model, retention and deletion terms, which vendor staff can listen, and multifactor authentication on every portal login.\",\"source\":\"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"Booking access varies by integration: none (messages only), read only, read and write, or a shared staff login. Legend NT advises getting a written list of what the tool reads and writes and how to switch access off, and testing the practice's real appointment types (hygiene recall, new-patient exam, limited emergency exam) rather than relying on a vendor demo.\",\"source\":\"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"Legend NT recommends a two-week pilot on a slice of calls (after-hours or overflow) while staff still answer the main line, measuring answered-call rate, correct bookings, transfers to staff, emergency handoffs and patient complaints, with success criteria agreed with the vendor beforehand.\",\"source\":\"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"In a 2024 Talkdesk survey of 1,000 U.S. adults, 81% preferred a human for medical advice and 74% wanted to discuss personal health information with a person, but 42% were okay with AI scheduling routine appointments. Top worries were inaccurate answers (26%), data privacy (24%) and loss of the human touch (24%).\",\"source\":\"https://www.themorninggrind.com/p/the-risks-and-rewards-of-hiring-an-ai-helper-to-staff-your-phones\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"A vendor blog (Hillflare, a seller in this market) recommends AI for after-hours, lunch-hour, weekend and weekday-overflow calls and always-human handling for post-op or clinical-concern calls, billing disputes, callers asking for a human, and low-confidence calls. Whether new-patient calls during business hours go to AI is left as practice-dependent. This is vendor opinion, not an independent benchmark.\",\"source\":\"https://hillflare.com/en/blog/ai-receptionist-vs-human-front-desk-2026\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"Vendor case study (Viva, an AI dental receptionist): Soothing Dental reported $30,877 in production in 30 days, with Viva handling calls, texts, booking, confirmations, reschedules and after-hours and overflow communication. This is vendor-published marketing for one practice and may not generalize.\",\"source\":\"https://www.getviva.ai/how-soothing-dental-generated-30877-in-production-in-30-days-with-vivas-ai-dental-receptionist-case-study/\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"Pricing units differ by model: live answering services usually charge a base fee plus per-minute or per-call rates; AI receptionists charge per conversation, per minute or a flat monthly plan; hybrids charge a base plan plus agent time. Exact dollar prices for dental-specific tools vary widely and were not confirmed in this session, so quotes should be requested on the same call-volume assumptions.\",\"source\":\"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"Products in this market include dental-focused AI receptionists (Viva, Weave, Arini), general AI answering services (Smith.ai), and AI add-ons from phone platforms such as RingCentral's AI Receptionist. Which of these integrate with the practice's specific Dentrix version (Dentrix integrations typically go through Henry Schein One's partner program) must be confirmed with each vendor.\",\"source\":\"\",\"verified\":false},{\"claim\":\"Maryland also has its own health-data rules beyond HIPAA, including the Confidentiality of Medical Records Act (Health-General 4-301 and following) and the Maryland Personal Information Protection Act, so a counsel or compliance review should cover state law and not only the federal BAA. No separate Silver Spring or Montgomery County rule on AI call answering was identified.\",\"source\":\"\",\"verified\":false},{\"claim\":\"If the AI makes outbound calls or texts (reminders, recall campaigns), federal TCPA rules apply. The FCC ruled in 2024 that AI-generated voices count as artificial voices under the TCPA. Inbound-only answering is not directly covered.\",\"source\":\"\",\"verified\":false}],\"open_questions\":[\"What share of current missed calls are new-patient calls, and when do they occur (lunch, while the front-desk person is with a patient, after hours)? This determines whether an overflow or after-hours pilot captures most of the benefit.\",\"What is the all-in monthly cost (base plan, per-call or per-minute overage, Dentrix integration fee, setup) at the practice's real call volume, and does it fit the monthly budget?\",\"Does the chosen vendor have a supported, read-and-write integration with the practice's exact Dentrix version, and can new-patient bookings be set for staff approval?\",\"Will the vendor sign a BAA covering the exact plan, and what does the contract say about sub-processors, model training on recordings, retention and deletion on exit?\",\"Has Maryland counsel approved the recording and AI-disclosure greeting for all call routes, including transfers and after-hours?\",\"How do the dentists and the front-desk person feel about the change, and what will staff time actually be spent on once calls are handled?\",\"How will urgent calls (severe pain, swelling, trauma) be routed to the on-call dentist after hours, and has the vendor tested that path?\",\"What does the RingCentral contract allow for forwarding, call-flow and call-recording, and are there extra fees or technical limits for routing to a third-party AI?\",\"What is the contract term and cancellation policy, and can the practice leave without penalty if the pilot results fall short?\"]}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":3441,"promptTokenCount":5019,"thoughtsTokenCount":842,"totalTokenCount":8460},"invocationId":"e-a5d218f5-fb1f-4770-9b60-0e156ab54f34","author":"researcher","actions":{"stateDelta":{"evidence":{"decision":"Whether Bright Smile Family Dental should route its main phone line to an AI receptionist that answers, books into the schedule and records calls, instead of the current front-desk phone line.","options":["Do nothing: keep the current front-desk line and RingCentral setup as is.","Run a limited two-week AI pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with staff still answering the main line, then decide.","Replace the front-desk line with an AI receptionist for all calls, with a human handoff for urgent or clinical calls.","Use a live or hybrid answering service (agents, optionally with AI first) for overflow and after-hours instead of a full AI receptionist."],"facts":[{"claim":"Maryland is generally treated as an all-party-consent state for recording calls (Maryland Wiretap Act, Courts and Judicial Proceedings 10-402), so a recorded call usually needs every party's consent. This is why a greeting that discloses recording is needed on every route (daytime, overflow, after-hours, transfers). Counsel should confirm.","source":"","verified":false},{"claim":"Legend NT advises having counsel approve the greeting, any 'automated assistant' notice and the recording process before go-live, because recording rules differ by state and some states require all parties to consent.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true},{"claim":"If a vendor hears, records, transcribes or stores patient information for the practice, HIPAA generally requires a signed business associate agreement (BAA). Legend NT advises confirming the BAA covers the exact plan being purchased, not only a higher tier, and getting the signed copy before the first live call.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true},{"claim":"Legend NT lists data questions to put in writing: who the sub-processors are (speech-to-text, language model, hosting), whether recordings or transcripts train any model, retention and deletion terms, which vendor staff can listen, and multifactor authentication on every portal login.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true},{"claim":"Booking access varies by integration: none (messages only), read only, read and write, or a shared staff login. Legend NT advises getting a written list of what the tool reads and writes and how to switch access off, and testing the practice's real appointment types (hygiene recall, new-patient exam, limited emergency exam) rather than relying on a vendor demo.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true},{"claim":"Legend NT recommends a two-week pilot on a slice of calls (after-hours or overflow) while staff still answer the main line, measuring answered-call rate, correct bookings, transfers to staff, emergency handoffs and patient complaints, with success criteria agreed with the vendor beforehand.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true},{"claim":"In a 2024 Talkdesk survey of 1,000 U.S. adults, 81% preferred a human for medical advice and 74% wanted to discuss personal health information with a person, but 42% were okay with AI scheduling routine appointments. Top worries were inaccurate answers (26%), data privacy (24%) and loss of the human touch (24%).","source":"https://www.themorninggrind.com/p/the-risks-and-rewards-of-hiring-an-ai-helper-to-staff-your-phones","verified":true},{"claim":"A vendor blog (Hillflare, a seller in this market) recommends AI for after-hours, lunch-hour, weekend and weekday-overflow calls and always-human handling for post-op or clinical-concern calls, billing disputes, callers asking for a human, and low-confidence calls. Whether new-patient calls during business hours go to AI is left as practice-dependent. This is vendor opinion, not an independent benchmark.","source":"https://hillflare.com/en/blog/ai-receptionist-vs-human-front-desk-2026","verified":true},{"claim":"Vendor case study (Viva, an AI dental receptionist): Soothing Dental reported $30,877 in production in 30 days, with Viva handling calls, texts, booking, confirmations, reschedules and after-hours and overflow communication. This is vendor-published marketing for one practice and may not generalize.","source":"https://www.getviva.ai/how-soothing-dental-generated-30877-in-production-in-30-days-with-vivas-ai-dental-receptionist-case-study/","verified":true},{"claim":"Pricing units differ by model: live answering services usually charge a base fee plus per-minute or per-call rates; AI receptionists charge per conversation, per minute or a flat monthly plan; hybrids charge a base plan plus agent time. Exact dollar prices for dental-specific tools vary widely and were not confirmed in this session, so quotes should be requested on the same call-volume assumptions.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true},{"claim":"Products in this market include dental-focused AI receptionists (Viva, Weave, Arini), general AI answering services (Smith.ai), and AI add-ons from phone platforms such as RingCentral's AI Receptionist. Which of these integrate with the practice's specific Dentrix version (Dentrix integrations typically go through Henry Schein One's partner program) must be confirmed with each vendor.","source":"","verified":false},{"claim":"Maryland also has its own health-data rules beyond HIPAA, including the Confidentiality of Medical Records Act (Health-General 4-301 and following) and the Maryland Personal Information Protection Act, so a counsel or compliance review should cover state law and not only the federal BAA. No separate Silver Spring or Montgomery County rule on AI call answering was identified.","source":"","verified":false},{"claim":"If the AI makes outbound calls or texts (reminders, recall campaigns), federal TCPA rules apply. The FCC ruled in 2024 that AI-generated voices count as artificial voices under the TCPA. Inbound-only answering is not directly covered.","source":"","verified":false}],"open_questions":["What share of current missed calls are new-patient calls, and when do they occur (lunch, while the front-desk person is with a patient, after hours)? This determines whether an overflow or after-hours pilot captures most of the benefit.","What is the all-in monthly cost (base plan, per-call or per-minute overage, Dentrix integration fee, setup) at the practice's real call volume, and does it fit the monthly budget?","Does the chosen vendor have a supported, read-and-write integration with the practice's exact Dentrix version, and can new-patient bookings be set for staff approval?","Will the vendor sign a BAA covering the exact plan, and what does the contract say about sub-processors, model training on recordings, retention and deletion on exit?","Has Maryland counsel approved the recording and AI-disclosure greeting for all call routes, including transfers and after-hours?","How do the dentists and the front-desk person feel about the change, and what will staff time actually be spent on once calls are handled?","How will urgent calls (severe pain, swelling, trauma) be routed to the on-call dentist after hours, and has the vendor tested that path?","What does the RingCentral contract allow for forwarding, call-flow and call-recording, and are there extra fees or technical limits for routing to a third-party AI?","What is the contract term and cancellation policy, and can the practice leave without penalty if the pilot results fall short?"]}},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"decision":"Whether Bright Smile Family Dental should route its main phone line to an AI receptionist that answers, books into the schedule and records calls, instead of the current front-desk phone line.","options":["Do nothing: keep the current front-desk line and RingCentral setup as is.","Run a limited two-week AI pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with staff still answering the main line, then decide.","Replace the front-desk line with an AI receptionist for all calls, with a human handoff for urgent or clinical calls.","Use a live or hybrid answering service (agents, optionally with AI first) for overflow and after-hours instead of a full AI receptionist."],"facts":[{"claim":"Maryland is generally treated as an all-party-consent state for recording calls (Maryland Wiretap Act, Courts and Judicial Proceedings 10-402), so a recorded call usually needs every party's consent. This is why a greeting that discloses recording is needed on every route (daytime, overflow, after-hours, transfers). Counsel should confirm.","source":"","verified":false},{"claim":"Legend NT advises having counsel approve the greeting, any 'automated assistant' notice and the recording process before go-live, because recording rules differ by state and some states require all parties to consent.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true},{"claim":"If a vendor hears, records, transcribes or stores patient information for the practice, HIPAA generally requires a signed business associate agreement (BAA). Legend NT advises confirming the BAA covers the exact plan being purchased, not only a higher tier, and getting the signed copy before the first live call.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true},{"claim":"Legend NT lists data questions to put in writing: who the sub-processors are (speech-to-text, language model, hosting), whether recordings or transcripts train any model, retention and deletion terms, which vendor staff can listen, and multifactor authentication on every portal login.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true},{"claim":"Booking access varies by integration: none (messages only), read only, read and write, or a shared staff login. Legend NT advises getting a written list of what the tool reads and writes and how to switch access off, and testing the practice's real appointment types (hygiene recall, new-patient exam, limited emergency exam) rather than relying on a vendor demo.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true},{"claim":"Legend NT recommends a two-week pilot on a slice of calls (after-hours or overflow) while staff still answer the main line, measuring answered-call rate, correct bookings, transfers to staff, emergency handoffs and patient complaints, with success criteria agreed with the vendor beforehand.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true},{"claim":"In a 2024 Talkdesk survey of 1,000 U.S. adults, 81% preferred a human for medical advice and 74% wanted to discuss personal health information with a person, but 42% were okay with AI scheduling routine appointments. Top worries were inaccurate answers (26%), data privacy (24%) and loss of the human touch (24%).","source":"https://www.themorninggrind.com/p/the-risks-and-rewards-of-hiring-an-ai-helper-to-staff-your-phones","verified":true},{"claim":"A vendor blog (Hillflare, a seller in this market) recommends AI for after-hours, lunch-hour, weekend and weekday-overflow calls and always-human handling for post-op or clinical-concern calls, billing disputes, callers asking for a human, and low-confidence calls. Whether new-patient calls during business hours go to AI is left as practice-dependent. This is vendor opinion, not an independent benchmark.","source":"https://hillflare.com/en/blog/ai-receptionist-vs-human-front-desk-2026","verified":true},{"claim":"Vendor case study (Viva, an AI dental receptionist): Soothing Dental reported $30,877 in production in 30 days, with Viva handling calls, texts, booking, confirmations, reschedules and after-hours and overflow communication. This is vendor-published marketing for one practice and may not generalize.","source":"https://www.getviva.ai/how-soothing-dental-generated-30877-in-production-in-30-days-with-vivas-ai-dental-receptionist-case-study/","verified":true},{"claim":"Pricing units differ by model: live answering services usually charge a base fee plus per-minute or per-call rates; AI receptionists charge per conversation, per minute or a flat monthly plan; hybrids charge a base plan plus agent time. Exact dollar prices for dental-specific tools vary widely and were not confirmed in this session, so quotes should be requested on the same call-volume assumptions.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true},{"claim":"Products in this market include dental-focused AI receptionists (Viva, Weave, Arini), general AI answering services (Smith.ai), and AI add-ons from phone platforms such as RingCentral's AI Receptionist. Which of these integrate with the practice's specific Dentrix version (Dentrix integrations typically go through Henry Schein One's partner program) must be confirmed with each vendor.","source":"","verified":false},{"claim":"Maryland also has its own health-data rules beyond HIPAA, including the Confidentiality of Medical Records Act (Health-General 4-301 and following) and the Maryland Personal Information Protection Act, so a counsel or compliance review should cover state law and not only the federal BAA. No separate Silver Spring or Montgomery County rule on AI call answering was identified.","source":"","verified":false},{"claim":"If the AI makes outbound calls or texts (reminders, recall campaigns), federal TCPA rules apply. The FCC ruled in 2024 that AI-generated voices count as artificial voices under the TCPA. Inbound-only answering is not directly covered.","source":"","verified":false}],"open_questions":["What share of current missed calls are new-patient calls, and when do they occur (lunch, while the front-desk person is with a patient, after hours)? This determines whether an overflow or after-hours pilot captures most of the benefit.","What is the all-in monthly cost (base plan, per-call or per-minute overage, Dentrix integration fee, setup) at the practice's real call volume, and does it fit the monthly budget?","Does the chosen vendor have a supported, read-and-write integration with the practice's exact Dentrix version, and can new-patient bookings be set for staff approval?","Will the vendor sign a BAA covering the exact plan, and what does the contract say about sub-processors, model training on recordings, retention and deletion on exit?","Has Maryland counsel approved the recording and AI-disclosure greeting for all call routes, including transfers and after-hours?","How do the dentists and the front-desk person feel about the change, and what will staff time actually be spent on once calls are handled?","How will urgent calls (severe pain, swelling, trauma) be routed to the on-call dentist after hours, and has the vendor tested that path?","What does the RingCentral contract allow for forwarding, call-flow and call-recording, and are there extra fees or technical limits for routing to a third-party AI?","What is the contract term and cancellation policy, and can the practice leave without penalty if the pilot results fall short?"]},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/researcher@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/researcher@1"]},"id":"4df97d15-ba40-4bec-9665-91710069ae04","timestamp":1794029298.9804738}

data: {"invocationId":"e-a5d218f5-fb1f-4770-9b60-0e156ab54f34","author":"crucible","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":"ROUND 1 — give your independent opinion. You have not seen the other seats yet.","nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/convene@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/convene@1"]},"id":"528a830c-c39a-4103-9acb-9609e1f76c5e","timestamp":1794029329.5944178}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"stance\":\"conditional\",\"headline\":\"Pilot after-hours and overflow first; do not replace the front desk this month.\",\"summary\":\"Do not sign up for a full AI receptionist this month. The risks are real: Maryland recording consent (F1), HIPAA business associate agreement (F3), and Dentrix booking access (F5) all need answers first. A two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls (F6) would show whether the tool books correctly and hands off urgent calls, while staff keep the main line. Patients also prefer humans for health matters (F7), so keep clinical and urgent calls with people.\",\"key_points\":[\"Start with a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with agreed success criteria (F6, F8).\",\"Make counsel approve the recording and AI-disclosure greeting on every route before go-live (F1, F2).\",\"Get a signed BAA covering the exact plan before the first live call (F3).\"],\"risks\":[\"Recording without all-party consent under Maryland law, or a greeting that misses transfers and after-hours routes (F1).\",\"Wrong bookings or unsupported Dentrix write access, creating scheduling errors (F5, F11).\",\"Urgent dental calls mishandled after hours, with no tested path to the on-call dentist (open question).\"],\"confidence\":70,\"pushback\":\"Are most missed calls really new-patient calls at times the front desk cannot answer? If the misses are mostly during business hours, a pilot on after-hours calls captures little of the benefit.\",\"tools\":[]}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":501,"promptTokenCount":4272,"totalTokenCount":4773},"invocationId":"e-a5d218f5-fb1f-4770-9b60-0e156ab54f34","author":"ai_tools_expert_r1","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Pilot after-hours and overflow first; do not replace the front desk this month.","summary":"Do not sign up for a full AI receptionist this month. The risks are real: Maryland recording consent (F1), HIPAA business associate agreement (F3), and Dentrix booking access (F5) all need answers first. A two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls (F6) would show whether the tool books correctly and hands off urgent calls, while staff keep the main line. Patients also prefer humans for health matters (F7), so keep clinical and urgent calls with people.","key_points":["Start with a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with agreed success criteria (F6, F8).","Make counsel approve the recording and AI-disclosure greeting on every route before go-live (F1, F2).","Get a signed BAA covering the exact plan before the first live call (F3)."],"risks":["Recording without all-party consent under Maryland law, or a greeting that misses transfers and after-hours routes (F1).","Wrong bookings or unsupported Dentrix write access, creating scheduling errors (F5, F11).","Urgent dental calls mishandled after hours, with no tested path to the on-call dentist (open question)."],"confidence":70,"pushback":"Are most missed calls really new-patient calls at times the front desk cannot answer? If the misses are mostly during business hours, a pilot on after-hours calls captures little of the benefit.","tools":[]},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/ai_tools_expert_r1@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/ai_tools_expert_r1@1"]},"branch":"ai_tools_expert_r1@1","id":"53bfc7f0-57bf-4ad3-a4a6-cd8f2f6f591e","timestamp":1794029329.6151235}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"stance\":\"conditional\",\"headline\":\"Pilot the AI on after-hours and overflow calls first; don't replace the front desk yet.\",\"summary\":\"Dental peers usually see the most gain from AI on after-hours, lunch and overflow calls, with a human still on urgent and clinical calls. A full replacement this month skips the checks that matter: recording consent, a signed BAA, Dentrix booking access, and real cost. Run a two-week pilot with agreed success measures, then decide.\",\"key_points\":[\"Staff still answer the main line during the pilot, so new-patient calls keep a human voice (F6, F8).\",\"Patients prefer humans for health topics, but 42% accept AI for routine scheduling, so keep clinical calls with staff (F7).\",\"Get the BAA, recording greeting, and Dentrix read-write access in writing before any live call (F2, F3, F5).\"],\"risks\":[\"Recording without all-party consent on every route, including transfers and after-hours (F1, F2).\",\"Bookings that break the schedule or misplace new patients if Dentrix integration is partial (F5, F11).\",\"Cost above the $400 budget once per-minute overage and setup fees are added (F10).\"],\"confidence\":72,\"pushback\":\"Assumes missed calls are mostly after-hours or overflow. If most missed new-patient calls come during business hours while staff are busy, a pilot on overflow alone will miss the main benefit (open question on call timing).; The pilot's value depends on measuring it honestly, so set pass/fail numbers with the vendor before signing.\"}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":511,"promptTokenCount":3745,"totalTokenCount":4256},"invocationId":"e-a5d218f5-fb1f-4770-9b60-0e156ab54f34","author":"industry_expert_r1","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Pilot the AI on after-hours and overflow calls first; don't replace the front desk yet.","summary":"Dental peers usually see the most gain from AI on after-hours, lunch and overflow calls, with a human still on urgent and clinical calls. A full replacement this month skips the checks that matter: recording consent, a signed BAA, Dentrix booking access, and real cost. Run a two-week pilot with agreed success measures, then decide.","key_points":["Staff still answer the main line during the pilot, so new-patient calls keep a human voice (F6, F8).","Patients prefer humans for health topics, but 42% accept AI for routine scheduling, so keep clinical calls with staff (F7).","Get the BAA, recording greeting, and Dentrix read-write access in writing before any live call (F2, F3, F5)."],"risks":["Recording without all-party consent on every route, including transfers and after-hours (F1, F2).","Bookings that break the schedule or misplace new patients if Dentrix integration is partial (F5, F11).","Cost above the $400 budget once per-minute overage and setup fees are added (F10)."],"confidence":72,"pushback":"Assumes missed calls are mostly after-hours or overflow. If most missed new-patient calls come during business hours while staff are busy, a pilot on overflow alone will miss the main benefit (open question on call timing).; The pilot's value depends on measuring it honestly, so set pass/fail numbers with the vendor before signing."},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/industry_expert_r1@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/industry_expert_r1@1"]},"branch":"industry_expert_r1@1","id":"3cec60a2-984a-42e3-a636-ef1946858025","timestamp":1794029329.613761}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"stance\":\"oppose\",\"headline\":\"Don't replace the front desk line this month; pilot after-hours calls first.\",\"summary\":\"The owner wants to switch the whole front-desk line to an AI receptionist this month. The evidence points to a narrower, slower start: a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls, with written contract terms. Recording consent, a signed BAA, Dentrix booking access and urgent-call routing are all unresolved. Signing now risks patient-data and legal problems before anyone has measured the missed-call gain.\",\"key_points\":[\"F6 recommends a two-week pilot on after-hours or overflow calls with agreed success criteria, which tests the idea without betting the main line.\",\"F3 and F2 say a signed BAA covering the exact plan and counsel-approved recording greetings must exist before the first live call; neither is confirmed yet.\",\"F7 shows most adults prefer a human for health topics (81%), and 42% accept AI for routine scheduling, so a full swap may fit only part of your call mix.\"],\"risks\":[\"Recording without all-party consent in Maryland (F1, unverified) could create legal exposure on every call route.\",\"Bad Dentrix booking writes or unapproved new-patient bookings could double-book chairs or misplace patient records before anyone notices.\",\"Urgent dental calls after hours might not reach the on-call dentist if the handoff is untested, and the cost may exceed the $400 budget once overage fees apply (unverified).\"],\"confidence\":72,\"pushback\":\"Assumes missed new-patient calls are the main leak. Nobody has counted how many missed calls are new patients or when they occur; if most are lunch or overflow, a cheaper live answering service may beat any AI tool.](Confirm with your accountant and attorney before signing; the Small Business Development Center can help with vendor comparison.)\"}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":607,"promptTokenCount":3802,"totalTokenCount":4409},"invocationId":"e-a5d218f5-fb1f-4770-9b60-0e156ab54f34","author":"devils_advocate_r1","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"stance":"oppose","headline":"Don't replace the front desk line this month; pilot after-hours calls first.","summary":"The owner wants to switch the whole front-desk line to an AI receptionist this month. The evidence points to a narrower, slower start: a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls, with written contract terms. Recording consent, a signed BAA, Dentrix booking access and urgent-call routing are all unresolved. Signing now risks patient-data and legal problems before anyone has measured the missed-call gain.","key_points":["F6 recommends a two-week pilot on after-hours or overflow calls with agreed success criteria, which tests the idea without betting the main line.","F3 and F2 say a signed BAA covering the exact plan and counsel-approved recording greetings must exist before the first live call; neither is confirmed yet.","F7 shows most adults prefer a human for health topics (81%), and 42% accept AI for routine scheduling, so a full swap may fit only part of your call mix."],"risks":["Recording without all-party consent in Maryland (F1, unverified) could create legal exposure on every call route.","Bad Dentrix booking writes or unapproved new-patient bookings could double-book chairs or misplace patient records before anyone notices.","Urgent dental calls after hours might not reach the on-call dentist if the handoff is untested, and the cost may exceed the $400 budget once overage fees apply (unverified)."],"confidence":72,"pushback":"Assumes missed new-patient calls are the main leak. Nobody has counted how many missed calls are new patients or when they occur; if most are lunch or overflow, a cheaper live answering service may beat any AI tool.](Confirm with your accountant and attorney before signing; the Small Business Development Center can help with vendor comparison.)"},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/devils_advocate_r1@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/devils_advocate_r1@1"]},"branch":"devils_advocate_r1@1","id":"f6e3e3ca-ca52-41bc-9776-560e524fb02d","timestamp":1794029329.6205392}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"stance\":\"conditional\",\"headline\":\"Pilot the AI receptionist on after-hours and overflow first; don't replace the main line yet.\",\"summary\":\"Don't switch the main line this month. Sign a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with staff still answering the main line. Before go-live, get a signed BAA covering the exact plan, written data terms, and counsel approval of the recording greeting. Then decide on full replacement using measured results.\",\"key_points\":[\"The pilot tests the exact slice where missed calls likely happen, and it is reversible (F6, F8).\",\"Patient trust is the weak point: 74% want health details handled by a person, though 42% accept AI for routine scheduling (F7).\",\"Signing a full-replacement contract now locks in the vendor before the Dentrix integration, data terms and recording compliance are proven (F3, F4, F5).\"],\"risks\":[\"Recording without all-party consent on transfers or after-hours routes, since Maryland is generally treated as all-party consent (F1, F2; counsel must confirm).\",\"Booking errors or urgent calls mishandled if the Dentrix write access and emergency handoff are not tested with real appointment types (F5, F8).\",\"Vendor savings claims are marketing, not benchmarks (F9), so the $400/month budget may be exceeded once per-call overage and integration fees are added (F10, open question).\"],\"confidence\":72,\"pushback\":\"Assumes missed calls are mostly after-hours or overflow. If most missed new-patient calls happen during business hours on the main line, a pilot on overflow captures little of the benefit, and the full decision cannot be avoided.\"}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":740,"promptTokenCount":3749,"thoughtsTokenCount":200,"totalTokenCount":4489},"invocationId":"e-a5d218f5-fb1f-4770-9b60-0e156ab54f34","author":"strategist_r1","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Pilot the AI receptionist on after-hours and overflow first; don't replace the main line yet.","summary":"Don't switch the main line this month. Sign a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with staff still answering the main line. Before go-live, get a signed BAA covering the exact plan, written data terms, and counsel approval of the recording greeting. Then decide on full replacement using measured results.","key_points":["The pilot tests the exact slice where missed calls likely happen, and it is reversible (F6, F8).","Patient trust is the weak point: 74% want health details handled by a person, though 42% accept AI for routine scheduling (F7).","Signing a full-replacement contract now locks in the vendor before the Dentrix integration, data terms and recording compliance are proven (F3, F4, F5)."],"risks":["Recording without all-party consent on transfers or after-hours routes, since Maryland is generally treated as all-party consent (F1, F2; counsel must confirm).","Booking errors or urgent calls mishandled if the Dentrix write access and emergency handoff are not tested with real appointment types (F5, F8).","Vendor savings claims are marketing, not benchmarks (F9), so the $400/month budget may be exceeded once per-call overage and integration fees are added (F10, open question)."],"confidence":72,"pushback":"Assumes missed calls are mostly after-hours or overflow. If most missed new-patient calls happen during business hours on the main line, a pilot on overflow captures little of the benefit, and the full decision cannot be avoided."},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/strategist_r1@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/strategist_r1@1"]},"branch":"strategist_r1@1","id":"5287fd28-23d7-464a-a861-2deb14666c36","timestamp":1794029329.6197011}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"stance\":\"conditional\",\"headline\":\"Pilot after-hours and overflow calls first; do not replace the front desk this month.\",\"summary\":\"The goal is sound: stop missing new-patient calls and free up the front desk. But full replacement this month skips required steps. Maryland recording consent (F1, unverified), a signed HIPAA business associate agreement (F3), and a Dentrix booking check (F5) must come first. Start with a two-week after-hours and overflow pilot under written success criteria (F6), keeping staff on the main line. Cost is unconfirmed (F10), so get quotes on your real call volume before signing.\",\"key_points\":[\"Maryland recording consent and a HIPAA BAA must be in place before the first live call (F1, F3).\",\"A two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls matches the vendor-recommended approach and limits exposure (F6, F8).\",\"Monthly cost against the $400 budget is unknown until quotes are based on real call volume (F10).\"],\"risks\":[\"Recording a call without all-party consent could violate Maryland law; the greeting must be counsel-approved (F1, F2, unverified).\",\"Patient data flowing to vendor sub-processors without a signed BAA or clear retention terms creates HIPAA and state-law exposure (F3, F4, F12).\",\"Urgent or clinical calls mishandled after hours could harm patients; the on-call handoff must be tested (F8).\"],\"confidence\":72,\"pushback\":\"Assumes the new-patient call loss is large enough to justify the cost. Check first how many missed calls are new-patient calls and when they happen; the answer may favor a cheaper answering service.\",\"checks\":[{\"area\":\"legal\",\"status\":\"block\",\"note\":\"Recording consent under Maryland's all-party rule (F1, unverified) must be confirmed by Maryland counsel for every call route before go-live.\"},{\"area\":\"compliance\",\"status\":\"block\",\"note\":\"A signed BAA covering the exact purchased plan is required before live calls if the vendor hears or stores patient information (F3); state health-data rules also need review (F12, unverified; counsel to confirm).\"},{\"area\":\"compliance\",\"status\":\"flag\",\"note\":\"Sub-processors, model training on recordings, retention and deletion, and MFA must be confirmed in writing (F4).\"},{\"area\":\"financial\",\"status\":\"flag\",\"note\":\"All-in monthly cost versus the $400 budget is unconfirmed; quotes on the same call volume are needed (F10).\"},{\"area\":\"operational\",\"status\":\"flag\",\"note\":\"Dentrix integration and read/write booking scope are unverified for the exact Dentrix version; new-patient bookings should require staff approval (F5, F11).\"},{\"area\":\"goals\",\"status\":\"pass\",\"note\":\"A pilot on after-hours and overflow calls addresses the missed new-patient calls and frees front-desk time without touching the main line (F6, F8).\"}]}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":948,"promptTokenCount":4139,"totalTokenCount":5087},"invocationId":"e-a5d218f5-fb1f-4770-9b60-0e156ab54f34","author":"realist_r1","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Pilot after-hours and overflow calls first; do not replace the front desk this month.","summary":"The goal is sound: stop missing new-patient calls and free up the front desk. But full replacement this month skips required steps. Maryland recording consent (F1, unverified), a signed HIPAA business associate agreement (F3), and a Dentrix booking check (F5) must come first. Start with a two-week after-hours and overflow pilot under written success criteria (F6), keeping staff on the main line. Cost is unconfirmed (F10), so get quotes on your real call volume before signing.","key_points":["Maryland recording consent and a HIPAA BAA must be in place before the first live call (F1, F3).","A two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls matches the vendor-recommended approach and limits exposure (F6, F8).","Monthly cost against the $400 budget is unknown until quotes are based on real call volume (F10)."],"risks":["Recording a call without all-party consent could violate Maryland law; the greeting must be counsel-approved (F1, F2, unverified).","Patient data flowing to vendor sub-processors without a signed BAA or clear retention terms creates HIPAA and state-law exposure (F3, F4, F12).","Urgent or clinical calls mishandled after hours could harm patients; the on-call handoff must be tested (F8)."],"confidence":72,"pushback":"Assumes the new-patient call loss is large enough to justify the cost. Check first how many missed calls are new-patient calls and when they happen; the answer may favor a cheaper answering service.","checks":[{"area":"legal","status":"block","note":"Recording consent under Maryland's all-party rule (F1, unverified) must be confirmed by Maryland counsel for every call route before go-live."},{"area":"compliance","status":"block","note":"A signed BAA covering the exact purchased plan is required before live calls if the vendor hears or stores patient information (F3); state health-data rules also need review (F12, unverified; counsel to confirm)."},{"area":"compliance","status":"flag","note":"Sub-processors, model training on recordings, retention and deletion, and MFA must be confirmed in writing (F4)."},{"area":"financial","status":"flag","note":"All-in monthly cost versus the $400 budget is unconfirmed; quotes on the same call volume are needed (F10)."},{"area":"operational","status":"flag","note":"Dentrix integration and read/write booking scope are unverified for the exact Dentrix version; new-patient bookings should require staff approval (F5, F11)."},{"area":"goals","status":"pass","note":"A pilot on after-hours and overflow calls addresses the missed new-patient calls and frees front-desk time without touching the main line (F6, F8)."}]},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/realist_r1@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/realist_r1@1"]},"branch":"realist_r1@1","id":"67cf10db-d21b-4379-9965-35ba909febd6","timestamp":1794029329.6185539}

