month. Sign a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with staff still answering the main line. Before go-live, get a signed BAA covering the exact plan, written data terms, and counsel approval of the recording greeting. Then decide on full replacement using measured results.","key_points":["The pilot tests the exact slice where missed calls likely happen, and it is reversible (F6, F8).","Patient trust is the weak point: 74% want health details handled by a person, though 42% accept AI for routine scheduling (F7).","Signing a full-replacement contract now locks in the vendor before the Dentrix integration, data terms and recording compliance are proven (F3, F4, F5)."],"risks":["Recording without all-party consent on transfers or after-hours routes, since Maryland is generally treated as all-party consent (F1, F2; counsel must confirm).","Booking errors or urgent calls mishandled if the Dentrix write access and emergency handoff are not tested with real appointment types (F5, F8).","Vendor savings claims are marketing, not benchmarks (F9), so the $400/month budget may be exceeded once per-call overage and integration fees are added (F10, open question)."],"confidence":72,"pushback":"Assumes missed calls are mostly after-hours or overflow. If most missed new-patient calls happen during business hours on the main line, a pilot on overflow captures little of the benefit, and the full decision cannot be avoided."}},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/round1@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/round1@1"]},"id":"0e72e616-8f41-40fd-9f9b-197d66b4017d","timestamp":1794029334.5501204}

data: {"invocationId":"e-a5d218f5-fb1f-4770-9b60-0e156ab54f34","author":"crucible","actions":{"stateDelta":{"round1":{"industry_expert":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Pilot the AI on after-hours and overflow calls first; don't replace the front desk yet.","summary":"Dental peers usually see the most gain from AI on after-hours, lunch and overflow calls, with a human still on urgent and clinical calls. A full replacement this month skips the checks that matter: recording consent, a signed BAA, Dentrix booking access, and real cost. Run a two-week pilot with agreed success measures, then decide.","key_points":["Staff still answer the main line during the pilot, so new-patient calls keep a human voice (F6, F8).","Patients prefer humans for health topics, but 42% accept AI for routine scheduling, so keep clinical calls with staff (F7).","Get the BAA, recording greeting, and Dentrix read-write access in writing before any live call (F2, F3, F5)."],"risks":["Recording without all-party consent on every route, including transfers and after-hours (F1, F2).","Bookings that break the schedule or misplace new patients if Dentrix integration is partial (F5, F11).","Cost above the $400 budget once per-minute overage and setup fees are added (F10)."],"confidence":72,"pushback":"Assumes missed calls are mostly after-hours or overflow. If most missed new-patient calls come during business hours while staff are busy, a pilot on overflow alone will miss the main benefit (open question on call timing).; The pilot's value depends on measuring it honestly, so set pass/fail numbers with the vendor before signing."},"ai_tools_expert":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Pilot after-hours and overflow first; do not replace the front desk this month.","summary":"Do not sign up for a full AI receptionist this month. The risks are real: Maryland recording consent (F1), HIPAA business associate agreement (F3), and Dentrix booking access (F5) all need answers first. A two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls (F6) would show whether the tool books correctly and hands off urgent calls, while staff keep the main line. Patients also prefer humans for health matters (F7), so keep clinical and urgent calls with people.","key_points":["Start with a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with agreed success criteria (F6, F8).","Make counsel approve the recording and AI-disclosure greeting on every route before go-live (F1, F2).","Get a signed BAA covering the exact plan before the first live call (F3)."],"risks":["Recording without all-party consent under Maryland law, or a greeting that misses transfers and after-hours routes (F1).","Wrong bookings or unsupported Dentrix write access, creating scheduling errors (F5, F11).","Urgent dental calls mishandled after hours, with no tested path to the on-call dentist (open question)."],"confidence":70,"pushback":"Are most missed calls really new-patient calls at times the front desk cannot answer? If the misses are mostly during business hours, a pilot on after-hours calls captures little of the benefit.","tools":[]},"realist":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Pilot after-hours and overflow calls first; do not replace the front desk this month.","summary":"The goal is sound: stop missing new-patient calls and free up the front desk. But full replacement this month skips required steps. Maryland recording consent (F1, unverified), a signed HIPAA business associate agreement (F3), and a Dentrix booking check (F5) must come first. Start with a two-week after-hours and overflow pilot under written success criteria (F6), keeping staff on the main line. Cost is unconfirmed (F10), so get quotes on your real call volume before signing.","key_points":["Maryland recording consent and a HIPAA BAA must be in place before the first live call (F1, F3).","A two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls matches the vendor-recommended approach and limits exposure (F6, F8).","Monthly cost against the $400 budget is unknown until quotes are based on real call volume (F10)."],"risks":["Recording a call without all-party consent could violate Maryland law; the greeting must be counsel-approved (F1, F2, unverified).","Patient data flowing to vendor sub-processors without a signed BAA or clear retention terms creates HIPAA and state-law exposure (F3, F4, F12).","Urgent or clinical calls mishandled after hours could harm patients; the on-call handoff must be tested (F8)."],"confidence":72,"pushback":"Assumes the new-patient call loss is large enough to justify the cost. Check first how many missed calls are new-patient calls and when they happen; the answer may favor a cheaper answering service.","checks":[{"area":"legal","status":"block","note":"Recording consent under Maryland's all-party rule (F1, unverified) must be confirmed by Maryland counsel for every call route before go-live."},{"area":"compliance","status":"block","note":"A signed BAA covering the exact purchased plan is required before live calls if the vendor hears or stores patient information (F3); state health-data rules also need review (F12, unverified; counsel to confirm)."},{"area":"compliance","status":"flag","note":"Sub-processors, model training on recordings, retention and deletion, and MFA must be confirmed in writing (F4)."},{"area":"financial","status":"flag","note":"All-in monthly cost versus the $400 budget is unconfirmed; quotes on the same call volume are needed (F10)."},{"area":"operational","status":"flag","note":"Dentrix integration and read/write booking scope are unverified for the exact Dentrix version; new-patient bookings should require staff approval (F5, F11)."},{"area":"goals","status":"pass","note":"A pilot on after-hours and overflow calls addresses the missed new-patient calls and frees front-desk time without touching the main line (F6, F8)."}]},"strategist":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Pilot the AI receptionist on after-hours and overflow first; don't replace the main line yet.","summary":"Don't switch the main line this month. Sign a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with staff still answering the main line. Before go-live, get a signed BAA covering the exact plan, written data terms, and counsel approval of the recording greeting. Then decide on full replacement using measured results.","key_points":["The pilot tests the exact slice where missed calls likely happen, and it is reversible (F6, F8).","Patient trust is the weak point: 74% want health details handled by a person, though 42% accept AI for routine scheduling (F7).","Signing a full-replacement contract now locks in the vendor before the Dentrix integration, data terms and recording compliance are proven (F3, F4, F5)."],"risks":["Recording without all-party consent on transfers or after-hours routes, since Maryland is generally treated as all-party consent (F1, F2; counsel must confirm).","Booking errors or urgent calls mishandled if the Dentrix write access and emergency handoff are not tested with real appointment types (F5, F8).","Vendor savings claims are marketing, not benchmarks (F9), so the $400/month budget may be exceeded once per-call overage and integration fees are added (F10, open question)."],"confidence":72,"pushback":"Assumes missed calls are mostly after-hours or overflow. If most missed new-patient calls happen during business hours on the main line, a pilot on overflow captures little of the benefit, and the full decision cannot be avoided."},"devils_advocate":{"stance":"oppose","headline":"Don't replace the front desk line this month; pilot after-hours calls first.","summary":"The owner wants to switch the whole front-desk line to an AI receptionist this month. The evidence points to a narrower, slower start: a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls, with written contract terms. Recording consent, a signed BAA, Dentrix booking access and urgent-call routing are all unresolved. Signing now risks patient-data and legal problems before anyone has measured the missed-call gain.","key_points":["F6 recommends a two-week pilot on after-hours or overflow calls with agreed success criteria, which tests the idea without betting the main line.","F3 and F2 say a signed BAA covering the exact plan and counsel-approved recording greetings must exist before the first live call; neither is confirmed yet.","F7 shows most adults prefer a human for health topics (81%), and 42% accept AI for routine scheduling, so a full swap may fit only part of your call mix."],"risks":["Recording without all-party consent in Maryland (F1, unverified) could create legal exposure on every call route.","Bad Dentrix booking writes or unapproved new-patient bookings could double-book chairs or misplace patient records before anyone notices.","Urgent dental calls after hours might not reach the on-call dentist if the handoff is untested, and the cost may exceed the $400 budget once overage fees apply (unverified)."],"confidence":72,"pushback":"Assumes missed new-patient calls are the main leak. Nobody has counted how many missed calls are new patients or when they occur; if most are lunch or overflow, a cheaper live answering service may beat any AI tool.](Confirm with your accountant and attorney before signing; the Small Business Development Center can help with vendor comparison.)"}}},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":"ROUND 2 — rebuttal. Below is every seat's Round 1 position. Respond to other seats by name. Change your stance only if a specific argument persuades you, and name it in changed_because. Do not drift toward agreement for its own sake. Return your full updated opinion.\n\n## Round 1\n\n### Industry Expert (industry_expert) — conditional, confidence 72\nHeadline: Pilot the AI on after-hours and overflow calls first; don't replace the front desk yet.\nSummary: Dental peers usually see the most gain from AI on after-hours, lunch and overflow calls, with a human still on urgent and clinical calls. A full replacement this month skips the checks that matter: recording consent, a signed BAA, Dentrix booking access, and real cost. Run a two-week pilot with agreed success measures, then decide.\nPoints: Staff still answer the main line during the pilot, so new-patient calls keep a human voice (F6, F8). | Patients prefer humans for health topics, but 42% accept AI for routine scheduling, so keep clinical calls with staff (F7). | Get the BAA, recording greeting, and Dentrix read-write access in writing before any live call (F2, F3, F5).\nRisks: Recording without all-party consent on every route, including transfers and after-hours (F1, F2). | Bookings that break the schedule or misplace new patients if Dentrix integration is partial (F5, F11). | Cost above the $400 budget once per-minute overage and setup fees are added (F10).\nPushback: Assumes missed calls are mostly after-hours or overflow. If most missed new-patient calls come during business hours while staff are busy, a pilot on overflow alone will miss the main benefit (open question on call timing).; The pilot's value depends on measuring it honestly, so set pass/fail numbers with the vendor before signing.\n\n### AI-Tools Expert (ai_tools_expert) — conditional, confidence 70\nHeadline: Pilot after-hours and overflow first; do not replace the front desk this month.\nSummary: Do not sign up for a full AI receptionist this month. The risks are real: Maryland recording consent (F1), HIPAA business associate agreement (F3), and Dentrix booking access (F5) all need answers first. A two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls (F6) would show whether the tool books correctly and hands off urgent calls, while staff keep the main line. Patients also prefer humans for health matters (F7), so keep clinical and urgent calls with people.\nPoints: Start with a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with agreed success criteria (F6, F8). | Make counsel approve the recording and AI-disclosure greeting on every route before go-live (F1, F2). | Get a signed BAA covering the exact plan before the first live call (F3).\nRisks: Recording without all-party consent under Maryland law, or a greeting that misses transfers and after-hours routes (F1). | Wrong bookings or unsupported Dentrix write access, creating scheduling errors (F5, F11). | Urgent dental calls mishandled after hours, with no tested path to the on-call dentist (open question).\nPushback: Are most missed calls really new-patient calls at times the front desk cannot answer? If the misses are mostly during business hours, a pilot on after-hours calls captures little of the benefit.\n\n### Realist (realist) — conditional, confidence 72\nHeadline: Pilot after-hours and overflow calls first; do not replace the front desk this month.\nSummary: The goal is sound: stop missing new-patient calls and free up the front desk. But full replacement this month skips required steps. Maryland recording consent (F1, unverified), a signed HIPAA business associate agreement (F3), and a Dentrix booking check (F5) must come first. Start with a two-week after-hours and overflow pilot under written success criteria (F6), keeping staff on the main line. Cost is unconfirmed (F10), so get quotes on your real call volume before signing.\nPoints: Maryland recording consent and a HIPAA BAA must be in place before the first live call (F1, F3). | A two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls matches the vendor-recommended approach and limits exposure (F6, F8). | Monthly cost against the $400 budget is unknown until quotes are based on real call volume (F10).\nRisks: Recording a call without all-party consent could violate Maryland law; the greeting must be counsel-approved (F1, F2, unverified). | Patient data flowing to vendor sub-processors without a signed BAA or clear retention terms creates HIPAA and state-law exposure (F3, F4, F12). | Urgent or clinical calls mishandled after hours could harm patients; the on-call handoff must be tested (F8).\nPushback: Assumes the new-patient call loss is large enough to justify the cost. Check first how many missed calls are new-patient calls and when they happen; the answer may favor a cheaper answering service.\nCheck [legal: block] Recording consent under Maryland's all-party rule (F1, unverified) must be confirmed by Maryland counsel for every call route before go-live.\nCheck [compliance: block] A signed BAA covering the exact purchased plan is required before live calls if the vendor hears or stores patient information (F3); state health-data rules also need review (F12, unverified; counsel to confirm).\nCheck [compliance: flag] Sub-processors, model training on recordings, retention and deletion, and MFA must be confirmed in writing (F4).\nCheck [financial: flag] All-in monthly cost versus the $400 budget is unconfirmed; quotes on the same call volume are needed (F10).\nCheck [operational: flag] Dentrix integration and read/write booking scope are unverified for the exact Dentrix version; new-patient bookings should require staff approval (F5, F11).\nCheck [goals: pass] A pilot on after-hours and overflow calls addresses the missed new-patient calls and frees front-desk time without touching the main line (F6, F8).\n\n### Strategist (strategist) — conditional, confidence 72\nHeadline: Pilot the AI receptionist on after-hours and overflow first; don't replace the main line yet.\nSummary: Don't switch the main line this month. Sign a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with staff still answering the main line. Before go-live, get a signed BAA covering the exact plan, written data terms, and counsel approval of the recording greeting. Then decide on full replacement using measured results.\nPoints: The pilot tests the exact slice where missed calls likely happen, and it is reversible (F6, F8). | Patient trust is the weak point: 74% want health details handled by a person, though 42% accept AI for routine scheduling (F7). | Signing a full-replacement contract now locks in the vendor before the Dentrix integration, data terms and recording compliance are proven (F3, F4, F5).\nRisks: Recording without all-party consent on transfers or after-hours routes, since Maryland is generally treated as all-party consent (F1, F2; counsel must confirm). | Booking errors or urgent calls mishandled if the Dentrix write access and emergency handoff are not tested with real appointment types (F5, F8). | Vendor savings claims are marketing, not benchmarks (F9), so the $400/month budget may be exceeded once per-call overage and integration fees are added (F10, open question).\nPushback: Assumes missed calls are mostly after-hours or overflow. If most missed new-patient calls happen during business hours on the main line, a pilot on overflow captures little of the benefit, and the full decision cannot be avoided.\n\n### Devil's Advocate (devils_advocate) — oppose, confidence 72\nHeadline: Don't replace the front desk line this month; pilot after-hours calls first.\nSummary: The owner wants to switch the whole front-desk line to an AI receptionist this month. The evidence points to a narrower, slower start: a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls, with written contract terms. Recording consent, a signed BAA, Dentrix booking access and urgent-call routing are all unresolved. Signing now risks patient-data and legal problems before anyone has measured the missed-call gain.\nPoints: F6 recommends a two-week pilot on after-hours or overflow calls with agreed success criteria, which tests the idea without betting the main line. | F3 and F2 say a signed BAA covering the exact plan and counsel-approved recording greetings must exist before the first live call; neither is confirmed yet. | F7 shows most adults prefer a human for health topics (81%), and 42% accept AI for routine scheduling, so a full swap may fit only part of your call mix.\nRisks: Recording without all-party consent in Maryland (F1, unverified) could create legal exposure on every call route. | Bad Dentrix booking writes or unapproved new-patient bookings could double-book chairs or misplace patient records before anyone notices. | Urgent dental calls after hours might not reach the on-call dentist if the handoff is untested, and the cost may exceed the $400 budget once overage fees apply (unverified).\nPushback: Assumes missed new-patient calls are the main leak. Nobody has counted how many missed calls are new patients or when they occur; if most are lunch or overflow, a cheaper live answering service may beat any AI tool.](Confirm with your accountant and attorney before signing; the Small Business Development Center can help with vendor comparison.)","nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/open_rebuttal@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/open_rebuttal@1"]},"id":"00dda3e0-b943-45c4-b4e0-043c428ab4d8","timestamp":1794029334.561698}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"changed_position\":false,\"changed_because\":\"\",\"rebuttals\":[{\"target\":\"devils_advocate\",\"their_claim\":\"A cheaper live answering service may beat any AI tool if most missed calls are at lunch or during overflow.\",\"response\":\"Agreed, and it belongs in the comparison, not dismissed. Get live and AI quotes on identical call volumes (F10) and let the missed-call timing count decide between them.\"},{\"target\":\"ai_tools_expert\",\"their_claim\":\"A pilot on after-hours calls captures little benefit if the misses happen during business hours.\",\"response\":\"Agreed on the risk. Add a business-hours overflow slice, when staff are with patients, so the pilot tests the real leak (F6, F8) instead of only the easy calls.\"}],\"concessions\":[\"A live or hybrid answering service must be priced next to AI, since it may win on cost for overflow calls (F10).\",\"The pilot must cover business-hours overflow as well as after-hours, not only after-hours calls (F6, F8).\"],\"stance\":\"conditional\",\"headline\":\"Pilot AI on overflow and after-hours calls, with written exit terms, before any full switch.\",\"summary\":\"Do not switch the main line this month. Next week, pull a count of missed calls by time of day and by new-patient status. Then run a two-week pilot on overflow and after-hours calls, with staff on the main line. Sign only after counsel approves the recording greeting, the vendor signs a BAA for the exact plan, and written data and exit terms are in hand. Decide on full replacement using pilot numbers, not vendor claims.\",\"key_points\":[\"The missed-call count sets the pilot slice; without it the pilot may test the wrong calls (open question, F6).\",\"Signing a full-replacement contract now locks in the vendor before Dentrix access, data terms and recording consent are proven (F2, F3, F4, F5).\",\"Patients accept AI for routine scheduling (42%) but mostly want a person for health topics (74%), so urgent and clinical calls need a tested human handoff (F7, F8).\"],\"risks\":[\"Recording without all-party consent on transfers or after-hours routes (F1, F2, unverified; counsel to confirm).\",\"Bad Dentrix writes or unapproved new-patient bookings double-booking chairs (F5, F11).\",\"Costs above the $400 budget once per-call overage and integration fees apply (F10).\"],\"confidence\":70,\"pushback\":\"Assume the missed new-patient calls are mostly overflow or after-hours; if they are mostly business-hours main-line misses, neither a pilot nor a cheap overflow service fixes the leak, and a full decision is unavoidable.\"}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":1966,"promptTokenCount":7428,"thoughtsTokenCount":1091,"totalTokenCount":9394},"invocationId":"e-a5d218f5-fb1f-4770-9b60-0e156ab54f34","author":"strategist_r2","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"changed_position":false,"changed_because":"","rebuttals":[{"target":"devils_advocate","their_claim":"A cheaper live answering service may beat any AI tool if most missed calls are at lunch or during overflow.","response":"Agreed, and it belongs in the comparison, not dismissed. Get live and AI quotes on identical call volumes (F10) and let the missed-call timing count decide between them."},{"target":"ai_tools_expert","their_claim":"A pilot on after-hours calls captures little benefit if the misses happen during business hours.","response":"Agreed on the risk. Add a business-hours overflow slice, when staff are with patients, so the pilot tests the real leak (F6, F8) instead of only the easy calls."}],"concessions":["A live or hybrid answering service must be priced next to AI, since it may win on cost for overflow calls (F10).","The pilot must cover business-hours overflow as well as after-hours, not only after-hours calls (F6, F8)."],"stance":"conditional","headline":"Pilot AI on overflow and after-hours calls, with written exit terms, before any full switch.","summary":"Do not switch the main line this month. Next week, pull a count of missed calls by time of day and by new-patient status. Then run a two-week pilot on overflow and after-hours calls, with staff on the main line. Sign only after counsel approves the recording greeting, the vendor signs a BAA for the exact plan, and written data and exit terms are in hand. Decide on full replacement using pilot numbers, not vendor claims.","key_points":["The missed-call count sets the pilot slice; without it the pilot may test the wrong calls (open question, F6).","Signing a full-replacement contract now locks in the vendor before Dentrix access, data terms and recording consent are proven (F2, F3, F4, F5).","Patients accept AI for routine scheduling (42%) but mostly want a person for health topics (74%), so urgent and clinical calls need a tested human handoff (F7, F8)."],"risks":["Recording without all-party consent on transfers or after-hours routes (F1, F2, unverified; counsel to confirm).","Bad Dentrix writes or unapproved new-patient bookings double-booking chairs (F5, F11).","Costs above the $400 budget once per-call overage and integration fees apply (F10)."],"confidence":70,"pushback":"Assume the missed new-patient calls are mostly overflow or after-hours; if they are mostly business-hours main-line misses, neither a pilot nor a cheap overflow service fixes the leak, and a full decision is unavoidable."},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/strategist_r2@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/strategist_r2@1"]},"branch":"strategist_r2@1","id":"4c8a6bed-b97c-4358-a3c5-8152f93623a1","timestamp":1794029334.5857136}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"changed_position\":false,\"changed_because\":\"\",\"rebuttals\":[{\"target\":\"realist\",\"their_claim\":\"A two-week after-hours and overflow pilot matches vendor advice and limits exposure.\",\"response\":\"Exposure is not limited: the pilot still records calls and handles patient data (F1, F3). F8 is vendor opinion, not a benchmark. Get the pilot's fees and exit terms in writing first.\"},{\"target\":\"industry_expert\",\"their_claim\":\"Peers see the most gain from AI on after-hours, lunch and overflow calls.\",\"response\":\"That gain claim rests on vendor sources (F8, F9), not independent data. If missed new-patient calls happen while the front desk is busy during business hours, an overflow pilot misses them.\"},{\"target\":\"strategist\",\"their_claim\":\"The pilot is reversible, so it is a low-risk test.\",\"response\":\"Reversible only if the contract allows a clean exit. Term and cancellation terms are unknown, and setup fees may be non-refundable. Reversibility is unverified until those terms are read.\"}],\"concessions\":[\"A signed BAA and counsel-approved recording greeting are required before any live call, pilot included (F2, F3).\"],\"stance\":\"oppose\",\"headline\":\"Pilot-first may test the wrong calls and still needs full compliance.\",\"summary\":\"The board's consensus is a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls. That may test the wrong calls. The advice behind it is vendor marketing (F8, F9), and nobody has counted missed new-patient calls by time of day. A pilot still records patient calls, so Maryland consent and a signed BAA (F1, F3) must be ready first. Pilot-first costs time and money and may not reach the real leak.\",\"key_points\":[\"The pilot is not low-risk: it still records calls and handles patient data, so consent and BAA gates apply (F1, F3).\",\"An overflow-only test may miss the main leak if new-patient calls are lost during business hours (open question on call timing).\",\"Vendor-set success criteria can flatter the tool; F9 is one practice's marketing, not a benchmark.\"],\"risks\":[\"Running two phone paths on RingCentral could misroute calls during the pilot; contract limits are unverified (F5, F11).\",\"Pilot minimums or setup fees could lock in cost with no clean exit (open question on contract term).\",\"Staff time spent on pilot setup and monitoring could cancel out the time the tool saves.\"],\"confidence\":70,\"pushback\":\"That a two-week overflow pilot is a cheap, safe test. It is neither if the contract has minimums or the call-timing data points to business-hours misses.\"}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":2781,"promptTokenCount":7490,"thoughtsTokenCount":1915,"totalTokenCount":10271},"invocationId":"e-a5d218f5-fb1f-4770-9b60-0e156ab54f34","author":"devils_advocate_r2","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"changed_position":false,"changed_because":"","rebuttals":[{"target":"realist","their_claim":"A two-week after-hours and overflow pilot matches vendor advice and limits exposure.","response":"Exposure is not limited: the pilot still records calls and handles patient data (F1, F3). F8 is vendor opinion, not a benchmark. Get the pilot's fees and exit terms in writing first."},{"target":"industry_expert","their_claim":"Peers see the most gain from AI on after-hours, lunch and overflow calls.","response":"That gain claim rests on vendor sources (F8, F9), not independent data. If missed new-patient calls happen while the front desk is busy during business hours, an overflow pilot misses them."},{"target":"strategist","their_claim":"The pilot is reversible, so it is a low-risk test.","response":"Reversible only if the contract allows a clean exit. Term and cancellation terms are unknown, and setup fees may be non-refundable. Reversibility is unverified until those terms are read."}],"concessions":["A signed BAA and counsel-approved recording greeting are required before any live call, pilot included (F2, F3)."],"stance":"oppose","headline":"Pilot-first may test the wrong calls and still needs full compliance.","summary":"The board's consensus is a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls. That may test the wrong calls. The advice behind it is vendor marketing (F8, F9), and nobody has counted missed new-patient calls by time of day. A pilot still records patient calls, so Maryland consent and a signed BAA (F1, F3) must be ready first. Pilot-first costs time and money and may not reach the real leak.","key_points":["The pilot is not low-risk: it still records calls and handles patient data, so consent and BAA gates apply (F1, F3).","An overflow-only test may miss the main leak if new-patient calls are lost during business hours (open question on call timing).","Vendor-set success criteria can flatter the tool; F9 is one practice's marketing, not a benchmark."],"risks":["Running two phone paths on RingCentral could misroute calls during the pilot; contract limits are unverified (F5, F11).","Pilot minimums or setup fees could lock in cost with no clean exit (open question on contract term).","Staff time spent on pilot setup and monitoring could cancel out the time the tool saves."],"confidence":70,"pushback":"That a two-week overflow pilot is a cheap, safe test. It is neither if the contract has minimums or the call-timing data points to business-hours misses."},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/devils_advocate_r2@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/devils_advocate_r2@1"]},"branch":"devils_advocate_r2@1","id":"a70dd29f-8c85-4d4b-ad6f-32b13b80dfed","timestamp":1794029334.5894387}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"changed_position\":false,\"changed_because\":\"\",\"rebuttals\":[{\"target\":\"devils_advocate\",\"their_claim\":\"A cheaper live answering service may beat any AI tool if missed calls are mostly lunch or overflow.\",\"response\":\"Agreed, a live or hybrid service is a real contender. Put it in the pilot comparison so the owner chooses on measured cost per booked new patient, not vendor claims (F9, F10).\"},{\"target\":\"realist\",\"their_claim\":\"Counsel, BAA and all-in cost must be confirmed before go-live, and new-patient losses must justify the cost.\",\"response\":\"I agree on the counsel and BAA gates. I would add that cost per booked new patient, not the monthly fee alone, is the real test. Quotes on actual call volume settle it (F10).\"},{\"target\":\"strategist\",\"their_claim\":\"If most missed new-patient calls happen on the main line during business hours, an overflow pilot captures little of the benefit.\",\"response\":\"Fair point, and it is why the pilot must log the time of day for every missed call first. If business-hour main-line misses dominate, widen the pilot scope rather than sign a full swap today.\"}],\"concessions\":[\"A live or hybrid answering service may beat full AI if missed calls are mostly lunch or overflow (devils_advocate, realist).\"],\"stance\":\"conditional\",\"headline\":\"Pilot AI on after-hours and overflow calls first; keep main line human.\",\"summary\":\"Peers usually gain most from AI on after-hours, lunch and overflow calls, with humans on urgent and clinical calls. Do not replace the front desk this month. First get counsel sign-off on recording consent, a signed BAA for the exact plan, and Dentrix booking access in writing. Then run a two-week pilot with agreed pass/fail numbers.\",\"key_points\":[\"Pilot slice tests after-hours and overflow misses while staff keep the main line (F6, F8).\",\"Patients want humans for health topics, but 42% accept AI scheduling, so keep clinical calls with staff (F7).\",\"Recording consent and a signed BAA must be settled before any live call (F1, F2, F3).\"],\"risks\":[\"Recording without all-party consent on transfers or after-hours routes (F1, unverified).\",\"Partial Dentrix integration causing misbooked new patients (F5, F11).\",\"All-in cost above the $400 budget after overage and setup fees (F10).\"],\"confidence\":72,\"pushback\":\"Are most missed new-patient calls at lunch or overflow, or during business hours on the main line? Count and time-stamp them first; if overflow dominates, a cheaper live or hybrid service may beat AI.\"}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":3429,"promptTokenCount":7430,"thoughtsTokenCount":2556,"totalTokenCount":10859},"invocationId":"e-a5d218f5-fb1f-4770-9b60-0e156ab54f34","author":"industry_expert_r2","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"changed_position":false,"changed_because":"","rebuttals":[{"target":"devils_advocate","their_claim":"A cheaper live answering service may beat any AI tool if missed calls are mostly lunch or overflow.","response":"Agreed, a live or hybrid service is a real contender. Put it in the pilot comparison so the owner chooses on measured cost per booked new patient, not vendor claims (F9, F10)."},{"target":"realist","their_claim":"Counsel, BAA and all-in cost must be confirmed before go-live, and new-patient losses must justify the cost.","response":"I agree on the counsel and BAA gates. I would add that cost per booked new patient, not the monthly fee alone, is the real test. Quotes on actual call volume settle it (F10)."},{"target":"strategist","their_claim":"If most missed new-patient calls happen on the main line during business hours, an overflow pilot captures little of the benefit.","response":"Fair point, and it is why the pilot must log the time of day for every missed call first. If business-hour main-line misses dominate, widen the pilot scope rather than sign a full swap today."}],"concessions":["A live or hybrid answering service may beat full AI if missed calls are mostly lunch or overflow (devils_advocate, realist)."],"stance":"conditional","headline":"Pilot AI on after-hours and overflow calls first; keep main line human.","summary":"Peers usually gain most from AI on after-hours, lunch and overflow calls, with humans on urgent and clinical calls. Do not replace the front desk this month. First get counsel sign-off on recording consent, a signed BAA for the exact plan, and Dentrix booking access in writing. Then run a two-week pilot with agreed pass/fail numbers.","key_points":["Pilot slice tests after-hours and overflow misses while staff keep the main line (F6, F8).","Patients want humans for health topics, but 42% accept AI scheduling, so keep clinical calls with staff (F7).","Recording consent and a signed BAA must be settled before any live call (F1, F2, F3)."],"risks":["Recording without all-party consent on transfers or after-hours routes (F1, unverified).","Partial Dentrix integration causing misbooked new patients (F5, F11).","All-in cost above the $400 budget after overage and setup fees (F10)."],"confidence":72,"pushback":"Are most missed new-patient calls at lunch or overflow, or during business hours on the main line? Count and time-stamp them first; if overflow dominates, a cheaper live or hybrid service may beat AI."},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/industry_expert_r2@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/industry_expert_r2@1"]},"branch":"industry_expert_r2@1","id":"73492cf2-950b-416d-b804-690ae8578bb8","timestamp":1794029334.5806684}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"changed_position\":false,\"changed_because\":\"\",\"rebuttals\":[{\"target\":\"devils_advocate\",\"their_claim\":\"A cheaper live answering service may beat any AI tool if missed calls are mostly lunch or overflow calls.\",\"response\":\"Agreed that a live service needs an equal quote in the same pilot. It may cost less, but it also needs a Dentrix booking path. Get both quotes on your real call volume before choosing.\"},{\"target\":\"realist\",\"their_claim\":\"Call timing and the monthly cost must be checked before the pilot is accepted.\",\"response\":\"Your legal and compliance gates match mine. Add one: Dentrix write access must be confirmed in writing, not assumed from a vendor demo (F5, F11).\"}],\"concessions\":[\"A live or hybrid answering service deserves an equal quote alongside AI, as the Devil's Advocate argues.\",\"Signing a full replacement now would lock in a vendor before data terms and recording rules are proven, as the Strategist warns (F3, F4).\"],\"stance\":\"conditional\",\"headline\":\"Pilot AI on after-hours calls first; price a live service too.\",\"summary\":\"Do not switch the main line this month. Run a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls with written success measures, and keep staff on the main line. Before go-live, get a signed BAA, a counsel-approved recording greeting, and Dentrix booking access confirmed in writing. Quote a live answering service alongside, since it may cost less. Have a Maryland attorney confirm the recording rules.\",\"key_points\":[\"The pilot covers the calls most likely missed, and it can be reversed (F6, F8).\",\"Maryland all-party consent makes a disclosure greeting a legal gate on every call route (F1, F2, unverified).\",\"Whether the tool can write bookings into Dentrix is unconfirmed by any vendor (F5, F11).\"],\"risks\":[\"Bookings written into Dentrix incorrectly, causing double-booked chairs (F5).\",\"Monthly cost above the $400 budget once per-minute overage and setup fees apply (F10).\",\"Urgent after-hours calls not reaching the on-call dentist because the handoff is untested (open question).\"],\"confidence\":70,\"pushback\":\"Assumes most missed new-patient calls happen after hours or during overflow; if they come at lunch or while staff are with patients, the pilot covers only a small slice.\",\"tools\":[{\"name\":\"RingCentral AI Receptionist\",\"use\":\"Answers and triages after-hours and overflow calls on the existing RingCentral line, so the pilot needs no new phone number.\",\"monthly_cost\":\"unknown; quote required (F10)\",\"works_with\":\"RingCentral (existing); Dentrix link unconfirmed (F11)\",\"caveat\":\"Dentrix booking write access is unverified; confirm the plan includes it and that it is month-to-month.\"},{\"name\":\"Smith.ai\",\"use\":\"Human-backed AI answering for overflow calls, taking messages and booking where a calendar link exists.\",\"monthly_cost\":\"unknown; quote required (F10)\",\"works_with\":\"Google Workspace calendar possible; Dentrix not confirmed (F11)\",\"caveat\":\"A general answering service, not built for dental scheduling; Dentrix booking is unverified and may need custom work.\"}]}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":3279,"promptTokenCount":7955,"thoughtsTokenCount":2195,"totalTokenCount":11234},"invocationId":"e-a5d218f5-fb1f-4770-9b60-0e156ab54f34","author":"ai_tools_expert_r2","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"changed_position":false,"changed_because":"","rebuttals":[{"target":"devils_advocate","their_claim":"A cheaper live answering service may beat any AI tool if missed calls are mostly lunch or overflow calls.","response":"Agreed that a live service needs an equal quote in the same pilot. It may cost less, but it also needs a Dentrix booking path. Get both quotes on your real call volume before choosing."},{"target":"realist","their_claim":"Call timing and the monthly cost must be checked before the pilot is accepted.","response":"Your legal and compliance gates match mine. Add one: Dentrix write access must be confirmed in writing, not assumed from a vendor demo (F5, F11)."}],"concessions":["A live or hybrid answering service deserves an equal quote alongside AI, as the Devil's Advocate argues.","Signing a full replacement now would lock in a vendor before data terms and recording rules are proven, as the Strategist warns (F3, F4)."],"stance":"conditional","headline":"Pilot AI on after-hours calls first; price a live service too.","summary":"Do not switch the main line this month. Run a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls with written success measures, and keep staff on the main line. Before go-live, get a signed BAA, a counsel-approved recording greeting, and Dentrix booking access confirmed in writing. Quote a live answering service alongside, since it may cost less. Have a Maryland attorney confirm the recording rules.","key_points":["The pilot covers the calls most likely missed, and it can be reversed (F6, F8).","Maryland all-party consent makes a disclosure greeting a legal gate on every call route (F1, F2, unverified).","Whether the tool can write bookings into Dentrix is unconfirmed by any vendor (F5, F11)."],"risks":["Bookings written into Dentrix incorrectly, causing double-booked chairs (F5).","Monthly cost above the $400 budget once per-minute overage and setup fees apply (F10).","Urgent after-hours calls not reaching the on-call dentist because the handoff is untested (open question)."],"confidence":70,"pushback":"Assumes most missed new-patient calls happen after hours or during overflow; if they come at lunch or while staff are with patients, the pilot covers only a small slice.","tools":[{"name":"RingCentral AI Receptionist","use":"Answers and triages after-hours and overflow calls on the existing RingCentral line, so the pilot needs no new phone number.","monthly_cost":"unknown; quote required (F10)","works_with":"RingCentral (existing); Dentrix link unconfirmed (F11)","caveat":"Dentrix booking write access is unverified; confirm the plan includes it and that it is month-to-month."},{"name":"Smith.ai","use":"Human-backed AI answering for overflow calls, taking messages and booking where a calendar link exists.","monthly_cost":"unknown; quote required (F10)","works_with":"Google Workspace calendar possible; Dentrix not confirmed (F11)","caveat":"A general answering service, not built for dental scheduling; Dentrix booking is unverified and may need custom work."}]},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/ai_tools_expert_r2@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/ai_tools_expert_r2@1"]},"branch":"ai_tools_expert_r2@1","id":"9663e3d4-7d75-4ae4-84c5-7ac3643d773e","timestamp":1794029334.5823874}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"changed_position\":false,\"changed_because\":\"\",\"rebuttals\":[{\"target\":\"devils_advocate\",\"their_claim\":\"A cheaper live answering service may beat any AI tool if most missed calls are lunch or overflow.\",\"response\":\"Fair test. Price both on the same call volume (F10). Both options are reversible, so compare them before choosing a vendor.\"},{\"target\":\"industry_expert\",\"their_claim\":\"A pilot on overflow calls misses the main benefit if most missed new-patient calls happen in business hours.\",\"response\":\"Agreed that the answer sets the scope. Count missed new-patient calls by time of day before picking the pilot slice.\"},{\"target\":\"strategist\",\"their_claim\":\"Signing a full-replacement contract now locks in the vendor before integration and data terms are proven (F3, F4, F5).\",\"response\":\"Agreed on sequence. Also confirm any pilot contract has no lock-in or cancellation penalty, since pricing is unconfirmed (F10).\"}],\"concessions\":[\"A cheaper live or hybrid answering service is a real option and should be priced alongside AI (F10).\",\"Pilot scope depends on when missed new-patient calls occur, so counting them comes first.\"],\"stance\":\"conditional\",\"headline\":\"Pilot after-hours and overflow calls only after legal and data checks clear.\",\"summary\":\"The goal is sound. Do not switch the main line this month. First, get Maryland counsel to approve the recording greeting on every route (F1, unverified), a signed BAA (F3), and written data terms (F4). Then run a two-week after-hours and overflow pilot with agreed pass-fail numbers (F6). Get quotes on your real call volume before signing (F10).\",\"key_points\":[\"Maryland recording consent is the first gate; counsel must confirm it for every route (F1, F2, unverified).\",\"Pilot on after-hours and overflow calls, with staff keeping the main line and agreed success measures (F6, F8).\",\"Cost against the $400 budget is unknown until quotes use your real call volume (F10).\"],\"risks\":[\"Recording without all-party consent on transfers or after-hours routes (F1, unverified).\",\"Patient data reaching vendor sub-processors without a signed BAA or clear retention terms (F3, F4, F12).\",\"Urgent calls after hours not reaching the on-call dentist if the handoff is untested (F8).\"],\"confidence\":70,\"pushback\":\"That missed new-patient calls are large and mostly after-hours or overflow; count them by time of day before deciding the pilot scope.\",\"checks\":[{\"area\":\"legal\",\"status\":\"block\",\"note\":\"Maryland all-party recording consent (F1, unverified) must be confirmed by Maryland counsel for every call route before go-live.\"},{\"area\":\"compliance\",\"status\":\"flag\",\"note\":\"A signed BAA covering the exact plan is needed before live patient calls (F3); Maryland health-data rules need counsel review (F12, unverified).\"},{\"area\":\"compliance\",\"status\":\"flag\",\"note\":\"Sub-processors, model training on recordings, retention, deletion and MFA must be confirmed in writing (F4).\"},{\"area\":\"financial\",\"status\":\"flag\",\"note\":\"All-in monthly cost versus the $400 budget is unconfirmed; quotes on the same call volume are needed (F10).\"},{\"area\":\"operational\",\"status\":\"flag\",\"note\":\"Dentrix version and read/write booking scope are unverified; new-patient bookings should need staff approval (F5, F11).\"},{\"area\":\"goals\",\"status\":\"pass\",\"note\":\"An after-hours and overflow pilot targets missed new-patient calls and front-desk time without touching the main line (F6, F8).\"}]}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":4009,"promptTokenCount":7804,"thoughtsTokenCount":2810,"totalTokenCount":11813},"invocationId":"e-a5d218f5-fb1f-4770-9b60-0e156ab54f34","author":"realist_r2","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"changed_position":false,"changed_because":"","rebuttals":[{"target":"devils_advocate","their_claim":"A cheaper live answering service may beat any AI tool if most missed calls are lunch or overflow.","response":"Fair test. Price both on the same call volume (F10). Both options are reversible, so compare them before choosing a vendor."},{"target":"industry_expert","their_claim":"A pilot on overflow calls misses the main benefit if most missed new-patient calls happen in business hours.","response":"Agreed that the answer sets the scope. Count missed new-patient calls by time of day before picking the pilot slice."},{"target":"strategist","their_claim":"Signing a full-replacement contract now locks in the vendor before integration and data terms are proven (F3, F4, F5).","response":"Agreed on sequence. Also confirm any pilot contract has no lock-in or cancellation penalty, since pricing is unconfirmed (F10)."}],"concessions":["A cheaper live or hybrid answering service is a real option and should be priced alongside AI (F10).","Pilot scope depends on when missed new-patient calls occur, so counting them comes first."],"stance":"conditional","headline":"Pilot after-hours and overflow calls only after legal and data checks clear.","summary":"The goal is sound. Do not switch the main line this month. First, get Maryland counsel to approve the recording greeting on every route (F1, unverified), a signed BAA (F3), and written data terms (F4). Then run a two-week after-hours and overflow pilot with agreed pass-fail numbers (F6). Get quotes on your real call volume before signing (F10).","key_points":["Maryland recording consent is the first gate; counsel must confirm it for every route (F1, F2, unverified).","Pilot on after-hours and overflow calls, with staff keeping the main line and agreed success measures (F6, F8).","Cost against the $400 budget is unknown until quotes use your real call volume (F10)."],"risks":["Recording without all-party consent on transfers or after-hours routes (F1, unverified).","Patient data reaching vendor sub-processors without a signed BAA or clear retention terms (F3, F4, F12).","Urgent calls after hours not reaching the on-call dentist if the handoff is untested (F8)."],"confidence":70,"pushback":"That missed new-patient calls are large and mostly after-hours or overflow; count them by time of day before deciding the pilot scope.","checks":[{"area":"legal","status":"block","note":"Maryland all-party recording consent (F1, unverified) must be confirmed by Maryland counsel for every call route before go-live."},{"area":"compliance","status":"flag","note":"A signed BAA covering the exact plan is needed before live patient calls (F3); Maryland health-data rules need counsel review (F12, unverified)."},{"area":"compliance","status":"flag","note":"Sub-processors, model training on recordings, retention, deletion and MFA must be confirmed in writing (F4)."},{"area":"financial","status":"flag","note":"All-in monthly cost versus the $400 budget is unconfirmed; quotes on the same call volume are needed (F10)."},{"area":"operational","status":"flag","note":"Dentrix version and read/write booking scope are unverified; new-patient bookings should need staff approval (F5, F11)."},{"area":"goals","status":"pass","note":"An after-hours and overflow pilot targets missed new-patient calls and front-desk time without touching the main line (F6, F8)."}]},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/realist_r2@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/realist_r2@1"]},"branch":"realist_r2@1","id":"daed6a59-7fb4-48f9-b890-83b489fc00e6","timestamp":1794029334.5837264}

